<!-- ════════════ SECTION 6 — KEY TAKEAWAYS ════════════ -->
<div id="takeaways" class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">Section 06</div>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
  </div>

  <ul class="takeaway-list">

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-blue">📈</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>Open Access is Accelerating Beyond Global Averages</h4>
        <p>The OA rate jumped 10 percentage points year-over-year (64.1% in 2024 → 74.1% in 2025), driven disproportionately by mega-journal growth. Scientific Reports, Heliyon, PLoS ONE, and Cureus collectively account for a structurally significant share of output.</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → Funders mandating OA compliance should expect easier enforcement going forward. Subscription-model publishers face accelerating disintermediation risk in the biomedical space.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-red">🌏</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>China Leads in Volume but Trails in Open Access Adoption</h4>
        <p>China accounts for 21.0% of indexed output — double the US share (10.7%) — but posts the lowest OA rate among top-10 producers (55.5%). All other top-10 countries maintain 68%–87% OA rates. Italy fields the largest teams (median 8 authors), signaling high multinational collaboration intensity.</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → Bilateral or multilateral OA agreements with Chinese institutions represent the single highest-leverage policy lever for global OA coverage gains. Targeted infrastructure investment (institutional repositories, APC waivers) warrants consideration.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-orange">🦠</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>COVID-19 Research Is Maturing — Post-Pandemic Topic Shift Underway</h4>
        <p>COVID-19 remains the top single MeSH major topic but its normalized rate declined from 2024 to 2025. Concurrently, neurological conditions (Alzheimer, Parkinson, EEG-linked research), oncology (Breast, Hepatocellular, Renal Cell neoplasms), and metabolic disease (Type 2 Diabetes) are consolidating as the dominant non-infectious clusters. Microbiome research (Gastrointestinal Microbiome: 16 records) signals growing fundamental science investment.</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → Portfolio rebalancing away from acute-infectious disease infrastructure toward chronic disease, neurodegenerative, and microbiome-focused programs is already occurring. Funders should model a 3–5 year ramp-down of COVID-specific line items.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-green">🤝</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>Collaboration Intensity is Stratified by Research Design</h4>
        <p>Preprints (mean 12.1 authors) and RCTs (mean 8.2) represent the most collaborative output types, while Editorials (mean 2.9) and Case Reports (mean 5.2) are predominantly single-institution work. The overall median of 6 authors per article reflects a mature multi-author norm across biomedical research.</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → Grant programs emphasizing large-team trial designs or consortium preprint output should expect and budget for higher author-overhead costs. Single-PI case-report pipelines can be supported with lighter infrastructure.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-purple">🧬</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>Emerging Acceleration Signals: AI/ML, PTSD, COPD, MicroRNAs</h4>
        <p>Among topics with ≥ 3 appearances in 2024, Deep Learning and Machine Learning both show higher normalized rates in 2025 — consistent with the sector-wide AI integration trend. Stress Disorders (PTSD) and COPD also show accelerating normalized rates, potentially reflecting post-pandemic mental health and respiratory sequelae research. MicroRNA research continues expanding its footprint (9 in 2024 → 11 total).</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → AI-in-biomedicine and digital health infrastructure funding should be treated as mission-critical rather than exploratory. PTSD/long-COVID respiratory research programs merit portfolio expansion ahead of anticipated volume growth.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="takeaway-icon icon-blue">⚠️</div>
      <div class="takeaway-body">
        <h4>High-Impact Study Types Remain Underrepresented in OA</h4>
        <p>Randomized Controlled Trials (43.1% OA) and Meta-Analyses (37.5% OA) — the two study designs most directly informing clinical practice — have the lowest open access rates of any research type. This creates a systematic gap between the highest-quality evidence and its accessibility to practitioners, especially in low-resource settings.</p>
        <p class="implication">Strategic Implication → Targeted OA mandates or APC subsidies specifically for RCTs and meta-analyses would yield outsized public health returns. Funders should consider differential OA requirements by study design rather than blanket policies.</p>
      </div>
    </li>

